#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106613} :activity/id #uuid "b632e7a8-8c52-5b89-bc1d-2f451e2f7e82" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050336 :topic/id #uuid "40dfe0e4-5817-55dc-af41-6963ede16db9" :topic/math-academy-id 1278} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107072 :tutorial/content "Consider the two vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,-2, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 3,0,-1 \\rangle$ shown in the picture below.\n\n![](images/87/87395f5fe6657cec5dd98eed9c3c535a5f6dc9f259d4558c86a4d618c904e5c1.png)\n\nTo find the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors, we can rearrange the dot product formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot |\\,b\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the magnitudes of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =3, \\\\ |\\,b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\Bigg( \\dfrac{1}{3\\sqrt{10}} \\Bigg) \\approx 83.9^\\circ.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "14af2112-1d41-5da4-8262-4c1541fee57b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2936 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "abecb670-0ed2-5399-a3bc-76a8818c1a52" :step/math-academy-id 15820 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105730 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218